{
  "dom_id": "step-q38231",
  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "Consider a linear transformation $T$ that maps ${R}^{2}$ onto the line $y=6x.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $⟨2,\\,12⟩\\in \\text{Im}(T)$\n\nII. $\\text{Ker}(T)=\\{0\\}$\n\nIII. $\\text{nullity}(T)=1$, where $T$ is the standard matrix of $T$",
  "worked_solution": "Recall that when the image of a linear transformation $T$ in ${R}^{2}$ with the standard matrix $T$ is a one-dimensional line, then\n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)=\\text{rank}(T)=1\n$$\n\nand, according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{nullity}(T) & =2-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =2-1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the point $(2,12)$ satisfies the equation of the line $y=6x$ since $12=6\\cdot 2.$ Hence, \n\n$$\n⟨2,\\,12⟩\\in \\text{Im}(T).\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $\\text{nullity}(T)=1,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne \\{0\\}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, we have that \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(T)=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
